  }
\exercise{1.4}{Batched matrices}{
\p{What if we wanted to perform int8[B,D] #{\cdot } int8[B,D,F] #{\to} int8[B,F] where we imagine having a different matrix for each batch element. What is the arithmetic intensity of this operation?}
}{\p{This dramatically reduces arithmetic intensity because it requires loading #{B \times } more bytes from memory. In particular, we now have #{BD + BDF + BF} total bytes moved. Even making the same approximation as before, this gives #{\frac{2BDF}{BDF} = 2}. Recall to saturate TPU we need this to be greater than #{240}...}}

\exercise{1.5}{Memory rooflines for GPUs}{
\p{For the H100 SXM, calculate the batch size at which a bfloat16 matrix multiplication will become compute-bound. Note that the Tensor Core FLOPs numbers are twice the true value since they’re only achievable with structured sparsity.}
}{\p{We only need to find two quantities in the spec sheet 1. the bf16 peak FLOPs/s and 2. the HBM bandwidth. From the spec sheet, a bf16 tensor core on the H100 sxm can achieve #{1.979e15} FLOPs / s and has a memory bandwidth of #{3.35e12} bytes / s. Using the approximation from above, we will become compute bound when #{B > \frac{1.979e15}{3.35e12} \approx 591}.}
\remark{
From the solution, the number we used was \em{with sparsity}. It turns out the true number is #{9.89e14}, which gives #{295} instead of #{590}.
}
}
